  <h1>How Nursing Home Neglect Claims Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Legal theories</h2><p>Negligence / medical malpractice: the facility failed to meet the standard of care. Elder abuse statute: many states have civil elder abuse laws providing enhanced damages, attorney fees, and in some cases a lower burden of proof. Wrongful death: when neglect causes death. Section 1983: for systemic constitutional violations at Medicaid-certified facilities.</p>
  <h2>Using regulatory violations as evidence</h2><p>CMS and state health departments inspect nursing homes and document deficiency citations (Form CMS-2567). Citations for the same deficiencies that caused the resident&rsquo;s harm are powerful evidence of the facility&rsquo;s knowledge and failure to correct. Request all inspection reports from the state health department and CMS&rsquo;s Care Compare database.</p>
  <h2>Expert witnesses</h2><p>Nursing home neglect cases almost always require expert testimony: a registered nurse or nursing home administrator on standard of care; a physician on causation; and an economist on damages. The plaintiff&rsquo;s attorney typically retains and pays for these experts out of the contingency fee.</p>
  <h2>Arbitration clauses</h2><p>Many nursing home admission agreements contain mandatory arbitration clauses. Some courts enforce them; others don&rsquo;t (when signed under duress by a family member without authority). If you signed an arbitration agreement, consult an attorney immediately — challenges must be raised early.</p>
